(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-book-questions-chapter-07-risk-from-disease-to-exposure-quiz"]={"title":"Chapter 7: Risk: From Disease to Exposure Quiz — Book Questions — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Book Questions","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"In a case-control study of oral contraceptives and myocardial infarction (heart attack), exposure to birth control pills was abstracted from medical records at the time of the myocardial infarction. Results might be biased toward finding an association by all of the following except:","choices":["Physicians might have asked about use of birth control pill more carefully in cases.","Having a myocardial infarction might have led to oral contraceptive use.","Physicians might have been more likely to record birth control use in cases.","Medical record abstractors might have looked for evidence of oral contraceptive use more carefully if they knew a patient had had a myocardial infarction.","Patients might have recalled exposure more readily when they had a heart attack."],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. If exposure to oral contraceptives was recorded just after the myocardial infarction, it could not have been a cause of it. All of the other choices could have artificially increased exposure in cases, resulting in a falsely elevated odds ratio. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 7.1"},{"q":"Investigators in Europe did a case-control study, nested in a multicountry cohort of more than 520,000 participants, of vitamin D concentration and the risk of colon cancer. They studied 1,248 cases of incident colon cancer arising in the cohort and an equal number of controls, sampled from the same cohort and matched by age, sex, and study center. Vitamin D was measured in blood samples taken years before diagnosis. Vitamin D levels were lower in patients with colon cancer, independent of a rich array of potentially confounding variables. The study results could be described by any of the following except:","choices":["Vitamin D deficiency was a cause of colorectal cancer.","Vitamin D levels were associated with colorectal cancer.","Vitamin D deficiency was a risk factor for colorectal cancer.","Nesting the study in a large cohort was a strength of the study.","The results might have been confounded with unmeasured variables related to vitamin D levels and colorectal cancer."],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Even an exemplary case-control study such as this one should not claim that it has identified a cause because unmeasured confounding is always possible. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 7.2"},{"q":"Which of the following is the most direct result of a case-control study?","choices":["Prevalence","Odds ratio","Risk difference","Relative risk","Incidence"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Case-control studies produce only odds ratios, which can be used to estimate relative risk. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 7.3"},{"q":"The epidemic curve for an acute infectious disease describes:","choices":["The usual incubation period for the causal agent","A comparison of illness over time in exposed versus nonexposed people","The onset of illness in cases over time","The duration of illness, on average, in affected individuals","The distribution of time from infection to first symptoms"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Epidemic curves describe the rise and fall in the number of cases over the time of the outbreak. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 7.4"},{"q":"Which of the following is the best reason for doing a case-control analysis of a cohort study?","choices":["Case-control studies are a feasible way of controlling for confounders not found in the cohort dataset.","Case-control studies can provide all the same information more easily.","Case-control studies can determine incidence of disease in exposed and nonexposed members of the cohort.","Case-control studies are in general stronger than cohort studies."],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. One can always obtain a crude relative risk from a cohort study. However, if the cohort data do not contain all of the variables that should be controlled for, a case-control analysis of the cohort study is a more efficient approach to including the additional data because the data needs to be collected only for cases and controls, not for the entire cohort. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 7.5"},{"q":"The best way to identify cases is to obtain them from:","choices":["A sample from the general (dynamic) population","Primary care physicians' offices","A community","A cohort representative of the population","A hospital"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It would be better to sample cases and controls from a cohort rather than a dynamic population, especially if exposure or disease is changing rapidly over time and if controls are not matched to the date of onset of disease for cases. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 7.6"},{"q":"What is the best reason to include multiple control groups in a case-control study?","choices":["To obtain a stronger estimate of relative risk","There are a limited number of cases and an ample number of potential controls","To control for confounding","To increase the generalizability of the result","The main control group may be systematically different from cases (other than on the exposure of interest)"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Multiple control groups (not to be confused with multiple controls per case) are a way of examining whether the results are “sensitive” to the types of controls chosen, that is, whether the results using the different control groups are substantially different, calling the results into question. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 7.7"},{"q":"A case-control study was conducted based on a registry of 10,000 children that were genetically predisposed to develop celiac disease (characterized by sensitivity to gluten in the diet that can lead to malabsorption). Investigators wanted to see if enterovirus infection was associated with increased risk of developing the disease in these children. The children were followed with regular blood and stool tests for 10 years. After 10 years, a sample of 100 cases was selected from 2,000 children who developed the disease, and 20% of these children had a preceding enterovirus infection. 200 Controls were selected from the remaining children without the disease, and 13% of these children had enterovirus diagnosed. Which of the following is true?","choices":["They crude odds ratio provides a good approximation of crude relative risk.","The sampling method for controls is inappropriate because only noncases are studied.","The crude odds ratio will overestimate relative risk.","The control selection will provide a direct estimate of population exposure."],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. All controls come from noncases in a case-control study, but sampling approaches vary. When noncases are selected using population-based methods throughout the study, the odds ratio is a direct estimate of relative risk. Noncases in this example are selected after study completion. When controls are selected this way, the odds ratio will closely approximate the relative risk only if the disease is relatively rare (less than 1% to 5%). As outcomes become more frequent, the odds ratio tends to overestimate relative risk when >1 (as in this case) and underestimate relative risk when <1. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 7.8"},{"q":"Case-control studies can be used to study all of the following except:","choices":["The early symptoms of stomach cancer","Risk factors for sudden infant death syndrome","The incidence of suicide in the adult population","The protective effect of aspirin","Modes of transmission of an infectious disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Case-control studies do not provide information on incidence (although if they are nested in a cohort, a cohort analysis of the same data can). — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 7.9"},{"q":"In a case-control study of exercise and sudden cardiac death, matching would be useful:","choices":["To control for all potential confounding variables in the study","To make cases and controls similar to each other with respect to a few major characteristics","To make it possible to examine the effects of the matched variables on estimated relative risk","To test whether the right controls were chosen for the cases in the study","To increase the generalizability of the study"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Matching is used to control for variables that might be strongly related to exposure or disease, to be sure that at least the cases and controls do not differ on those variables. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 7.10"},{"q":"In a case-control study of whether prolonged air travel is a risk factor for venous thromboembolism, 60 out of 100 cases and 40 out of 100 controls had prolonged air travel. What was the crude odds ratio from this study?","choices":["0.44","1.5","2.25","3.0","Not possible to calculate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The crude odds ratio is obtained by creating a 2 × 2 table relating the number of cases versus controls to the number of exposed versus nonexposed people and dividing the cross products. In this case, the odds ratio is 60 × 60 divided by 40 × 40 = 2.25. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 7.11"},{"q":"A population-based case-control study would be especially useful for studying:","choices":["The population attributable risk of disease","Multiple outcomes (diseases)","The incidence of rare diseases","The prevalence of disease","Risk factors for disease"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Case-control studies cannot study multiple outcomes because they begin with the presence or absence of only one disease, cannot report incidence, and cases should be incident (new onset), not prevalent. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 7.12"},{"q":"The prevalence odds ratio of rheumatoid arthritis provides an estimate of:","choices":["The relative risk of arthritis","The attributable risk of arthritis","Risk factors for the duration of arthritis","The association between a patient characteristic and prevalence of arthritis","Risk factors for the incidence of arthritis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Odds ratios based on prevalent cases can provide a rough measure of association but not a comparison of risk, which is about incident (new-onset) cases. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 7.13"},{"q":"A case-control study was conducted to determine if children with asthma who used inhaled corticosteroids are at increased risk of fractures. A national registry was used to select cases and population-based controls, and possible confounders assessed by phone interview and chart review. Which of the following is incorrect?","choices":["Matching on sex would remove confounding for this factor","If there is no difference between the crude and adjusted odds ratio, then confounding for the measured factors was not present","Incidence of fracture in children using corticosteroids cannot be estimated","This study can identify risk factors but not establish cause","Difference in rates of participation in recreational sports between cases and controls would show that control selection was biased"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Confounders may occur with different frequencies in cases and controls in the population under study. Confounding can be controlled for in the design or analysis stages. Selecting controls matched on a confounder is a method of removing the effects of a confounder for that characteristic. Case-control studies use odds ratios to estimate relative risk, but cannot estimate incidence. An association found in a case-control study can be one aspect of an argument for causation, but cannot by itself establish causation. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 7.14"},{"q":"In an outbreak of acute gastroenteritis, a case-control study would be especially useful for identifying:","choices":["Characteristics of the people affected","The number of people affected over time","The microbe or toxin causing the outbreak","The mode of transmission","Where the causative agent originated"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. During the early phases of an outbreak, the offending microbe or toxin is usually known, but even if it is not, the most pressing question is how the disease is being spread. This information can then be used to stop the outbreak and identify the source. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 7.15"},{"q":"Sampling cases and controls from a defined population or cohort accomplishes which of the following?","choices":["It tends to include cases and controls that are representative of the source population.","It is the only way of including incident (new) cases of disease.","It avoids the need for inclusion and exclusion criteria.","It matches cases and controls on important variables.","It ensures that the results are generalizable."],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. If a case-control study is based on all or a random sample of cases and a random sample of controls from a population or cohort, the cases and controls should be similar to the population cases and controls (though cases and controls may differ in some characteristics, as with cohort studies). — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 7.16"},{"q":"Case-control studies would be useful for answering all of the following questions except:","choices":["Do cholesterol-lowering drugs prevent coronary heart disease?","Are complications more common with fiberoptic cholecystectomy than with conventional (open) surgery?","Is drinking alcohol a risk factor for breast cancer?","How often do complications occur after fiberoptic cholecystectomy?","How effective are antibiotics for otitis media?"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Case-control studies do not provide information on incidence. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 7.17"},{"q":"In a case-control study of airplane flight and thrombophlebitis, all of the following conditions should be met for the odds ratio to be a reasonable estimate of relative risk except:","choices":["Controls were sampled from the same population as cases.","Cases and controls met the same inclusion and exclusion criteria.","Other variables that might be related to air travel and thrombosis were controlled for.","Cases and controls were equally susceptible to developing thrombophlebitis (e.g., were equally mobile, weight, recent trauma, previous VTE) other than for air travel.","The incidence of thrombophlebitis was more than 5/100."],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The odds ratio approximates the relative risk when the disease is rare; a rule of thumb is <1/100. For more common diseases the type of sampling should be population based for the odds ratio to approximate relative risk. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 7.18"}]};
